#: README.md:1
msgid "Using plumed benchmark"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:3
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:5
msgid ""
"Let's go through the options one by one, By inputting `plumed benchmark "
"--help` in the terminal we get"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:35
msgid ""
"[`--plumed`](#input-files) is used to pass one or more input files to the "
"benchmark"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:36
msgid "[`--kernel`](#kernels) is used to pass one or more plumed kernels"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:37
msgid "`--natoms` sets the number of atoms"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:38
msgid "`--nsteps` sets the number of steps"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:39
msgid ""
"[`--maxtime`](#time-limit) stops gracefully the benchmark after the a "
"certain amount of time has elapsed."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:40
msgid ""
"`--sleep` add a sleep timer during the loop, simulating an MD program doing "
"its calculations"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:41
msgid ""
"[`--atom-distribution`](#atom-distributions) chose between a few different "
"kinds of atom distributions: \"line\", \"cube\", \"sphere\", \"globs\" and "
"\"sc\""
msgstr ""

#: README.md:42
msgid ""
"[`--domain-decomposition`](#domain-decomposition-and-shuffled-atoms) "
"emulates a domain decomposition and forces the `--shuffled` option"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:43
msgid ""
"[`--shuffled`](#domain-decomposition-and-shuffled-atoms) shuffles the atom "
"indexes"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:44
msgid "`--dump-trajectory` dumps the trajectory to a file"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:47
msgid "A few words on the execution order."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:49
msgid ""
"When specifying more than 1 input file or more than 1 plumed kernels the "
"benchmark will store more than one kernel-file combination (see below "
"[here](#input-files),[here](#kernels) and [here](#combining---kernel-and---"
"plumed)) and run the benchmark on each one of these."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:51
msgid ""
"Each benchmark step will compute the atomic distribution only once and then "
"execute the plumed command chain with all the specified kernels, so if you "
"ask for 100 steps and 5 kernels and/or 5 input files, your machine will run "
"500 plumed steps."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:53
msgid ""
"The order of execution of the kernel-file combinations is randomly "
"determined at each benchmark step, this should remove skew due to the "
"execution order."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:55
msgid "In the following paragraphs, the more complex options are explained."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:57
msgid "Time limit"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:59
msgid ""
"With `--maxtime` you can control how much time the benchmark will run for. "
"This option is useful if you are using an HPC queue system as it ensures "
"that the benchmark will exit gracefully and then produce the final output "
"with the times report when the asked time has elapsed."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:61
msgid ""
"The input string is parsed by plumed, so you can even ask for \"pi\" "
"seconds, or, on a more practical example you can ask for "
"`--maxtime=\"60*5\"` to ask for 5 minutes of benchmark. **Remember the "
"quotes**, since the asterisk will be used as a wildcard by your shell."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:63
msgid "Input files"
msgstr "入力ファイル"

#: README.md:65
msgid ""
"With `--plumed` we can specify a series of input files, column separated, "
"like `--plumed \"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`, without any other "
"options this will run the \"this\" kernel as three separated instances "
"against the 3 input files"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:68 README.md:99 README.md:128 README.md:174
msgid "Examples"
msgstr "例"

#: README.md:71
msgid "`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\"`"
msgstr "`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\"`"

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:76 README.md:87 README.md:106 README.md:135 README.md:146
msgid "this"
msgstr ""

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:76 README.md:106 README.md:117 README.md:135 README.md:146
msgid "/plumed.dat/"
msgstr ""

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:77 README.md:88 README.md:136
msgid "./other.so"
msgstr ""

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:78 README.md:89 README.md:117 README.md:137 README.md:148
msgid "./new.so"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:82
msgid ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"coord.dat\"`"
msgstr ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"coord.dat\"`"

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:87 README.md:107 README.md:118 README.md:136 README.md:147
msgid "/coord.dat/"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:93
msgid "Kernels"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:94
msgid ""
"You can use the `--kernel` option to specify a series of **paths** to "
"various libplumedKernel.so or \"this\", \"this\" is the kernel \"bundled\" "
"with the plumed installation from which you are running plumed benchmark. "
"The benchmark will run the specified kernels (like `--kernel "
"\"this:./kenel1.so:../../src/lib/install/libplumedKernel.so`) against the "
"specified plumed file."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:97
msgid ""
"If you have moved the kernels in the working directory please specify the "
"kernel using `./kernelName.so`, as if you use `kernelName.so` plumed "
"benchmark searches for the so file in the LD_LIBRARY_PATH and not in your "
"local directory, usually this brings a crash, but if a library with the same"
" name is in the LD_LIBRARY_PATH that will be loaded!."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:101
msgid "`plumed benchmark --plumed \"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"
msgstr "`plumed benchmark --plumed \"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"

#. mermaid node label
#: README.md:108 README.md:119 README.md:137
msgid "/third.dat/"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:112
msgid ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"
msgstr ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"

#: README.md:123
msgid "Combining --kernel and --plumed"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:125
msgid ""
"You can specify multiple kernels **and** multiple input files together. In "
"this case, the number of kernels and input files must be equal: the kernel "
"in the first position will be used with the file in the first position, the "
"kernel in the second position will be used with the file in the second "
"position and so on."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:130
msgid ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"
msgstr ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:./other.so:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:third.dat\"`"

#: README.md:141
msgid ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:this:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:coord.dat\"`"
msgstr ""
"`plumed benchmark --kernel \"this:this:./new.so\" --plumed "
"\"plumed.dat:coord.dat:coord.dat\"`"

#: README.md:152
msgid "Domain decomposition and shuffled atoms"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:154
msgid ""
"The `--shuffled` option simply shuffles the indices of the atoms before "
"starting the benchmark. This will force plumed to adopt a more complex "
"communication with the \"MD engine\""
msgstr ""

#: README.md:156
msgid ""
"The `--domain-decomposition` option will emulate a domain decomposition from"
" an MD engine. With this option the `--shuffled` option is forced"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:158
msgid ""
"If the user wants to run a benchmark with more than 1 mpi threads (with "
"`mpirun -np x plumed benchmark ...`) the `--domain-decomposition` option and"
" the `--shuffled` options will be forced on automatically."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:161
msgid "Atom Distributions"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:162
msgid ""
"When specifying `--atom-distribution` you can choose one among \"line\", "
"\"cube\", \"sphere\", \"globs\" and \"sc\""
msgstr ""

#: README.md:164
msgid "The atom distribution can be divided into two categories:"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:165
msgid ""
"standard: the base is that each atom is in a grid with each atom at a "
"distance of 1 nm (so that the atom radius is 0.5nm)."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:166
msgid ""
"\"line\" the atoms are displaced in a line at 1nm from each other, then at "
"each step they are displaced in a sphere or radius 0.5nm around the initial "
"position"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:167
msgid ""
"\"sc\" the atoms are displaced in a simple cubic crystal, as of the time of "
"writing this there are no extra movements, the cube is always a perfect cube"
" (1,8,27,64...) but it is filled bottom-up with the number of atoms "
"requested"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:168
msgid ""
"Random distributions: the atoms are distributed uniformly with a density of "
"1 atom per nm^3 (the total volume is equal to the number of atoms). In these"
" distributions, the atoms are randomly displaced in the available volume at "
"each step, so they are **not** compatible with a neighbor list that updates "
"every n>1 steps :"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:169
msgid "\"sphere\" the atoms are uniformly distributed in a sphere"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:170
msgid "\"cube\" the atoms are uniformly distributed in a cube"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:171
msgid "\"globs\" the atoms are uniformly distributed in two spheres"
msgstr ""

#: README.md:175
msgid "In the examples here the atoms are visualized with a radius of 0.5nm."
msgstr ""

#: README.md:177
msgid ""
"In the cube, sphere and globs example, atom 0 is highlighted in red to show "
"that is randomly displaced at each step"
msgstr ""

